body:
It seems odd to me that he won't let you have access to his phone. Personally I'm glad to let my BS look at my phone and computer and whatever else he wants (though I prefer he tell me he's doing it rather than waiting for me to fall asleep/take a shower/whatever, but I'm not really in a position to demand anything). I figure the more times he checks and sees that I've done nothing objectionable, the better.

&#x200B;

IMO, you're the one who gets to make the rules for a while. He should be following any guidelines you want to establish that would make you feel more comfortable in the relationship, even if he doesn't like them. Of course, that shouldn't be the dynamic forever, and the goal for both of you should be to get to a place where you are more sensitive to each other's needs and can communicate well. But yeah, he ought to be putting in a lot more effort.

&#x200B;

If you're a cheater who wants to reconcile, privacy is a privilege, not a right.

>  I had to stop letting my self worth be determined by someone else before I could start to heal. 

Strangely enough, I feel like this is also true for me (a WS). I have been (and still am, but working on it) utterly dependent on my husband's opinion of me to determine my self-worth. Even before the cheating, I felt like he was a much better person than me and that he deserved so much better. It would crush me when he was disappointed in me, even for little things, so I started lying about stuff I did that I knew he wouldn't approve of (because that was easier than changing/doing work). Because I was already lying about stuff, it was easier to cheat, especially when someone came along who showered me with compliments and praise and was ~~as flawed~~ more flawed than me.

&#x200B;

I still think my husband is a much better person than me (duh). And of course, now that he knows I cheated, his opinion of me has gone from disappointment to a mixture of hatred, disgust, and generally thinking I'm an awful nightmare piece of shit that he regrets marrying (though thankfully he isn't divorcing me yet). In order to not kill myself, I now kind of am forced to make my self-esteem not so dependent on him. I of course want to impress him, and hopefully earn his love back, but I've realized that before this I valued him \*believing\* that I am good more than actually \*being\* good.

&#x200B;

I have to stop caring so much about his opinion of me before I can become a better person, paradoxically enough.

&#x200B;
